    {
      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-01-2018-june/q9",
      "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:90mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-01-2018-june/q09-figure.svg\" alt=\"Axes labelled North (y) and East (x) with origin O; a closed oval curve tilted upwards to the right around O; P is its furthest west point and Q its furthest east point, each with a vertical dashed tangent line.\"></figure><p>Figure 4 shows a sketch of the curve with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^2-2xy+3y^2=50\"></span></p><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=\\dfrac{y-x}{3y-x}\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol><p>The curve is used to model the shape of a cycle track with both <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> measured in km.</p><p>The points <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"Q\"></span> represent points that are furthest west and furthest east of the origin <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span>, as shown in Figure 4.</p><p>Using part (a),</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"2\"><li>find the exact coordinates of the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></li><li>Explain briefly how to find the coordinates of the point that is furthest north of the origin <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span>. (You <strong>do not</strong> need to carry out this calculation). <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li></ol>",
